[{"id":"VRCE-6-51-1","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":1,"english":"Hearing the lament of the king of Rākşasas, Kumbhakarma, laughing said.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.849,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051001","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c55305d22ee4c527ec91a54f1ac2efde4caab930fca9213a191cddbd983f8ef1","compact":"7284c4fe5a18f7e1321ed564889168c26264553ca25ec74e0bc67bb2c73d9fc6","nasal":"3dffe1c1392bb6f470493da96c27a84c10387a624d7dc02c135aa7c18161a81e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-2","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":2,"english":"That evil which on the occasion of the conclave we had seen, has befallen you, who have passed by what was fraught with your good.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051002","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"580aeaa7094fe5df3bfd4c23b1bc9244d73322f93d3fc23710791d03d558976e","compact":"34a15c6ce0f0d109f7eb948fdfbc0bc631a82ab53eaa12c89bba66c60c963954","nasal":"0112c6105f583567690689d63f267966ec03c8b511697bb386d18c1459726f17"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-3","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":3,"english":"Even as a sinner reaps perdition, verily has the fruit of your unrighteous act found you.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051003","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a7d078f209c8ef5676f8a5638c125be9f25142bbb5b23d8144edcc8b47bda0a8","compact":"a81b63a4cd2bda082f359030b79d88154c4c3a52e88b745e5ef12355f793b905","nasal":"a81b63a4cd2bda082f359030b79d88154c4c3a52e88b745e5ef12355f793b905"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-4","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":4,"english":"At first, O mighty king, you did not ponder over this course of conduct; nor, from pride of prowess, did you at all take note of the evil thereof.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.862,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051004","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5f8220df76d4703481cf812dd23af6de429464c2666a82bd6b2454ec0194d638","compact":"c3cfdf325bd16058f7aef73b09368800a6884452035d4d30b34a05ccfbe0e1e3","nasal":"ef638c0c74a213b84713303f528433deae8e1bc23a3fdbf469dcdf1f97ca4ab2"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-5","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":5,"english":"He that dose last what should be done first, and what should be done last, is ignorant of what is prudence and what not.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051005","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"846f4ab56cd4c44e4a30aa907e4d4887e32c0e7d0f36e4991343c5e111da18e7","compact":"2678139b0b722498f744aabf8ddf38ea584e9a94e89328bf8020b8a3ef378359","nasal":"2678139b0b722498f744aabf8ddf38ea584e9a94e89328bf8020b8a3ef378359"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-6","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":6,"english":"Acts not performed in harmony with season and place, like to acts performed counter to season and place, are the occasions of misery, and resemble clarified butter poured into fire that has not been sanctified.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051006","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8759d0bb8d9fc2ac4b889029092d4af8848dd59a0557b983462677e6eabee3fe","compact":"49804fbe08f0d0b3b09037906701f8389946a66ae54ba91ee0c6c1f706a2a8a6","nasal":"531d72e9fab5b456248f5854d6aa815d08ba4470237b81211eb7c8680276467c"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-7","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":7,"english":"He stay well in the path that, in consultation with his counsellors, ascertain the five way touching the three kinds of action* *The five ways are (1) the mans of commencing an act (2) person, thing and wealth, (3) division of season and place, (4) providing agains mischance (5) success. The three kinds of action are treaty, war, etc. All this is nebulous; And the commentator is sadly reticent over this extremely important passage.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.617,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051007","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"77c2997ebdf4545fd8404232089bc8e24fd4e6e06de8f8e8ea12f8449815cedb","compact":"923d152b6676bb98bc09939209a34ad59567f980c431eb334ddb59bb5e86932d","nasal":"571d2357b961da0e22f2698263409cae5bfea51e806b96edfe299c441e7befad"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-8","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":8,"english":"The king that aided by his own intelligence as well as his counsellors act after reflection in consonance with the science of polity, that distinguish his friends from his foes), and, O lord of the Raksasas, that seasonably' pursue righteousness, or profit, or desire, or any two, or all these combined-truly has understanding.? 1. Religion is to be sought in the morning, profit in the afternoon, and desire at nigh, so the commentator. 2. One is at sea in the midst of these formless generalities. The tantalized intellect makes a desperate attempt to grasp the body of the shadow seeming substance, but the empty air mocks his pains. A translator, however, is fast bound to the oar and must pull on, will be, nil he.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.7,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051008","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7f7691314e798ab16f4a9d68fb911f5de6ac8053351bc281259ec1bc3315af9c","compact":"ee4bf24a8fca6c9597b4147b8284713dcf8a6beed68914cee80ae07571f54f48","nasal":"ee4bf24a8fca6c9597b4147b8284713dcf8a6beed68914cee80ae07571f54f48"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-9","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":9,"english":"The king that aided by his own intelligence as well as his counsellors act after reflection in consonance with the science of polity, that distinguish his friends from his foes), and, O lord of the Raksasas, that seasonably' pursue righteousness, or profit, or desire, or any two, or all these combined-truly has understanding.? 1. Religion is to be sought in the morning, profit in the afternoon, and desire at nigh, so the commentator. 2. One is at sea in the midst of these formless generalities. The tantalized intellect makes a desperate attempt to grasp the body of the shadow seeming substance, but the empty air mocks his pains. A translator, however, is fast bound to the oar and must pull on, will be, nil he.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.781,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051009","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"80612aba3e04ea4aefaadd0c16a09d0ee70a52c1534259ecaefe8fd34ecee20a","compact":"7ddc9221cc75f52cf1d6ea1a7426f85aa52d20f3bbf6dedca37a00fa1296a02c","nasal":"842c83dc681838906f8396b034c1edaa488e01170025ad84755ba0340b479196"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-10","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":10,"english":"But the king or the heir apparent, that, having heard what is prime among the three, does not understand, has his time in vain on his erudition.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.773,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051010","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7a8e4b392b66e8ccd5d4953eb6128f35e27545bcc782ee245f61dcdd8c1c4cdb","compact":"b064a148bb24109cf2f530a35c0189f3d9fd4599971e541331e4eba071fe5555","nasal":"57efa0285c2f229fb0dc898143dd549159825e58f681e38befd66b2f71e4a4eb"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-11","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":11,"english":"He that controlling his own self, consult with his counsellors seasonably touching gift, forbearance, difference and prowess, as well as virtue, profit and desire-does not in this world come by calamity.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.852,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051011","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"88fdd8117eb6fc3ae160dd5ef8142bc585502cab31870012fbc7ff9cce0f3e01","compact":"8ac1cc37e248a081e344d4417323c6a91e464ee0b27b03b5a9879b3f6dedf750","nasal":"b02336dcd1198fd590067f00a3171cdfaa27b5201c5ab35799769140833f2448"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-12","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":12,"english":"He that controlling his own self, consult with his counsellors seasonably touching gift, forbearance, difference and prowess, as well as virtue, profit and desire-does not in this world come by calamity.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.735,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051012","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1a98bfa364893381e863dc61a1dc3b250f9f97269abcb108364c88799f257af2","compact":"fed6991a55d53e908e65d70110382b15281f489a858bdb1ecd708e682f335553","nasal":"3927790e4d7ce2b6be299822c1ef0e573bf8e55bcb723baa072e0b3867450dd1"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-13","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":13,"english":"Perceiving the fair fruit (of a line of action) accruing to him here, a king should act in consultation with counsellors endowed with intelligence and versed in the import of things.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.688,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051013","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"207be7a76a857ee6920445eda8d29bd37133d8fd2a3d231c381eed1afcf6ffe7","compact":"38b78d3b6d2d74ff7864152c192d4f5cf92226bbd364a1aef4b59945da6860d7","nasal":"4625e3b29ec2078d9e5f4273936b57c65848a78cc3235c450f48427dc61c044d"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-14","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":14,"english":"Not understanding the sense of the scriptures, persons possessed of beastly intelligence, entering into the heart of their counsellors, are inspired with a desire of speaking out of sheer volubility.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.904,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051014","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"07e91bdf905030d195accd037412e3643d0f889bf89902d114cc27698a68f76b","compact":"5f4b84247594a8c4422e885717ad8b6fbf9b42c7c3107d7fcd57825f852b684b","nasal":"c240c9ab918a4218ccfcd44bcd991f38fe67d07ceced40e9604fe11ad876cc2f"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-15","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":15,"english":"Albeit uttered heedfully, the speech of those that are ignorant of learning and the knowledge of the sense of things, and who are intent upon attaining abounding affluence, should not be acted upon.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.8,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051015","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"187fc5101ccd11f24f83409a5461b7bdc20225eaf40479e1e1a559df3baf1403","compact":"474535604470e40bf807ff2bf5d23d1091dbb3426b17009dc69228714c75a8c4","nasal":"474535604470e40bf807ff2bf5d23d1091dbb3426b17009dc69228714c75a8c4"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-16","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":16,"english":"Those-spoilers of business-who from recklessness talk of pernicious as if profitableshould certainly be rejected as counsellors.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.571,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051016","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2e5d75e1e105f21bff46094ac04d275f6f0b57c924b2d3bb8c915e1cfa11b0bc","compact":"df6f105b4c2c6d0b29b5eb3c2a3e6b35450dc5d275f381e0a98b58abc4677953","nasal":"f1009466d79a6f2becadc1190ec77fe0520e68b1a7895d18ffacdedf0f8ad004"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-17","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":17,"english":"Some counsellors-destroyers of their mastersjoining intelligent enemies (of their lords), act in a way opposed to their interests in this world.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-63-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6051017","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d5c5d5afa07b867fe95300985ff63f0a6df6bd19cc51a1b42a4166a64ef69ab0","compact":"4c25b5c4ef66690babc4e1ed253579054c6df3bb3e42992359c12a07f47cd6b1","nasal":"7b8e4fb4c5ec0cf16ceccedbdfaa40cc56f59e8cf22326d21881048489b6c3fd"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-51-18","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":51,"verse":18,"english":"Their lords, at the hour of consultation, by their behaviour should know these counsellorsenemies in the guise of friends-who have been won over (by the opposite party).","alignm